00:03:23,890 --> 00:03:28,115
If you didn't know that, delta is one of the primary Greeks tied to an options contract.

68
00:03:28,115 --> 00:03:31,980
If you buy a call, it means you're taking on a positive delta position.

69
00:03:31,980 --> 00:03:32,880
Now, what does that mean?

70
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:38,970
It means every time the underlying or the stock is increasing in value, so is your call option.

71
00:03:38,970 --> 00:03:43,735
For the most part, we're taking aside, we're not considering the other Greeks like volatility and stuff like that.

72
00:03:43,735 --> 00:03:45,995
We're just going to focus on delta for now.

73
00:03:45,995 --> 00:03:53,685
So if you buy a call and your delta is 0.30, a 0.30 delta, that means every time the stock goes up $1,

74
00:03:53,685 --> 00:03:58,175
your call is going to increase by 30 cents, which is $30 in your pocket.

75
00:03:58,175 --> 00:04:01,010
But we have to think about who sold you that call.

76
00:04:01,010 --> 00:04:03,565
There was a market maker more than likely attached.

77
00:04:03,565 --> 00:04:04,920
It could have been any other option, right?

78
00:04:04,920 --> 00:04:08,065
But for the most part, it's more than likely going to be a market maker.

79
00:04:08,065 --> 00:04:12,740
They're the ones that are there to sell any type of option, and it is their role to do so.

80
00:04:12,740 --> 00:04:16,510
They don't necessarily have much of a choice because they get a lot of perks for being market makers.

81
00:04:16,510 --> 00:04:21,700
But the downside to having all of those perks means they have to service our needs.

82
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:24,085
So in a sense, they're kind of a slave to the market.

83
00:04:24,085 --> 00:04:27,150
And if there's a lot of demand for a particular

84
